-- title:
--   Hecke operators $T_n$ on $S_k(\Gamma_1(M))$: coefficient formula, $T_mT_n=T_nT_m$, $T_{mn}=T_mT_n$, prime-power recursion, and $T_nf=a_n(f)f$ for normalized eigenforms
-- statement:
--   Let $M\ge1$ and $k$ be integers. For $f\in S_k(\Gamma_1(M))$ write $f=\sum_{m\ge0}a_m(f)q^m$, $q=e^{2\pi iz}$, and for $d\ge1$ prime to $M$ let $\langle d\rangle f=f|_k\gamma$ for any $\gamma=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d'\end{pmatrix}\in\Gamma_0(M)$ with $d'\equiv d\pmod M$ (the diamond operator; it does not depend on the choice of $\gamma$). Then for every $n\ge1$ there is a unique $\mathbb C$-linear map $T_n$ of $S_k(\Gamma_1(M))$ with
--
--   $$a_m(T_nf)=\sum_{d\mid\gcd(m,n),\ \gcd(d,M)=1}d^{k-1}\,a_{mn/d^2}\big(\langle d\rangle f\big)\qquad(m\ge0)$$
--
--   (the Hecke operator $T_n$; for a prime $p\mid M$ this is $U_p$, $a_m(U_pf)=a_{mp}(f)$), and these operators satisfy:
--
--   1. $T_mT_n=T_nT_m$ for all $m,n$;
--   2. $T_{mn}=T_mT_n$ whenever $\gcd(m,n)=1$;
--   3. $T_{p^{r+2}}=T_pT_{p^{r+1}}-p^{k-1}\langle p\rangle T_{p^r}$ for every prime $p\nmid M$ and $r\ge0$;
--   4. $T_{p^r}=T_p^{\,r}$ for every prime $p\mid M$ and $r\ge0$;
--   5. if $f$ has nebentypus $\chi$, i.e. $f|_k\gamma=\chi(d')f$ for every $\gamma=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d'\end{pmatrix}\in\Gamma_0(M)$, for a Dirichlet character $\chi$ mod $M$, then $a_m(T_nf)=\sum_{d\mid\gcd(m,n)}\chi(d)\,d^{k-1}a_{mn/d^2}(f)$ for all $n\ge1$, $m\ge0$; and if moreover $a_1(f)=1$ and $f$ is an eigenvector of every $T_p$ ($p$ prime, including $U_p$ for $p\mid M$), then $T_nf=a_n(f)\,f$ for every $n\ge1$.
--
--   Role: this is the basic structure of the Hecke algebra of $S_k(\Gamma_1(M))$ at every level, bad primes included: the $T_n$ commute, are generated by the $T_p$ and the diamond operators, and act explicitly on $q$-expansions; formally $\sum_nT_nn^{-s}=\prod_{p\nmid M}(1-T_pp^{-s}+p^{k-1}\langle p\rangle p^{-2s})^{-1}\prod_{p\mid M}(1-U_pp^{-s})^{-1}$. For a normalized eigenform with character $\chi$ the coefficients $a_n(f)$ are the eigenvalues of the $T_n$, which yields $a_{mn}=a_ma_n$ for coprime $m,n$ and $a_{p^{r+2}}=a_pa_{p^{r+1}}-\chi(p)p^{k-1}a_{p^r}$, so $L(f,s)$ has an Euler product. Here $T_n$ is characterized by its $q$-expansion action rather than as a double coset operator; for prime $n=p$ the formula is the classical one, $a_m(T_pf)=a_{mp}(f)+p^{k-1}a_{m/p}(\langle p\rangle f)$ ($p\nmid M$, the last term only when $p\mid m$).
--
--   **Formalization Note**: $S_k(\Gamma_1(M))$ is Mathlib's `CuspForm` for the image of `CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma1 M` in $GL_2(\mathbb R)$ (any `k : ℤ`; `[NeZero M]` means $M\ge1$), $a_m(f)$ is `(qExpansion 1 f).coeff m`, $f|_k\gamma$ is the slash action `⇑f ∣[k] γ` of `γ : SL(2, ℤ)`, and $d'$ is `γ 1 1`. The statement asserts the existence of two families `D T : ℕ → Module.End ℂ (S_k(Γ₁(M)))`. `D d` is the diamond operator $\langle d\rangle$ for every $d$ prime to $M$: it is characterized by the slash formula for every admissible $\gamma$ (such $\gamma$ exist, so `D d` is pinned down), and `D d` for $d$ not prime to $M$ never occurs. For $n\ge1$, `T n` is the unique linear map with the coefficient formula (`∀ T', P T' ↔ T' = T n`). The divisor sum is over `(Nat.gcd m n).divisors` filtered by `d.Coprime M`; `m * n / d ^ 2` is exact natural-number division, and `d ^ (k - 1)` is an integer power in ℂ. `T 0` plays no role: items 1 and 2 are stated for all natural numbers and hold with $T_0=1$ (in item 2, $\gcd(0,n)=1$ forces $n=1$). In item 3, $p^{k-1}\langle p\rangle T_{p^r}$ is `(p : ℂ) ^ (k - 1) • (D p * T (p ^ r))`, products being compositions. The nebentypus hypothesis of item 5 is `∀ γ ∈ Gamma0 M, ⇑f ∣[k] γ = χ (γ 1 1) • ⇑f` for `χ : DirichletCharacter ℂ M`; `χ d` for natural $d$ is $\chi$ at $d$ mod $M$ (zero when $\gcd(d,M)>1$), and "eigenvector of every $T_p$" is `∀ p, p.Prime → ∃ c, T p f = c • f`.
-- source:
--   F. Diamond and J. Shurman, A First Course in Modular Forms, Springer GTM 228 (2005), §5.2–5.3 (Propositions 5.2.4 and 5.3.1) and §5.8; T. Miyake, Modular Forms, Springer (1989), §4.5; G. Shimura, Introduction to the Arithmetic Theory of Automorphic Functions, Princeton (1971), Ch. 3

import Mathlib

set_option autoImplicit false

open UpperHalfPlane MatrixGroups ModularForm

theorem RamanujanPetersson.exists_heckeTn_gamma1_coeff_mul_primePow_eigen (M : ℕ) [NeZero M] (k : ℤ) :
    ∃ D T : ℕ → Module.End ℂ
        (CuspForm ((CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma1 M : Subgroup SL(2, ℤ)) : Subgroup (GL (Fin 2) ℝ)) k),
      (∀ d : ℕ, d.Coprime M → ∀ γ ∈ CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma0 M, ((γ 1 1 : ℤ) : ZMod M) = d →
        ∀ f, ⇑(D d f) = ⇑f ∣[k] γ) ∧
      (∀ n : ℕ, 0 < n → ∀ T' : Module.End ℂ
          (CuspForm ((CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma1 M : Subgroup SL(2, ℤ)) : Subgroup (GL (Fin 2) ℝ)) k),
        (∀ f (m : ℕ), (qExpansion 1 (T' f)).coeff m =
            ∑ d ∈ (Nat.gcd m n).divisors with d.Coprime M,
              (d : ℂ) ^ (k - 1) * (qExpansion 1 (D d f)).coeff (m * n / d ^ 2)) ↔ T' = T n) ∧
      (∀ m n : ℕ, T m * T n = T n * T m) ∧
      (∀ m n : ℕ, m.Coprime n → T (m * n) = T m * T n) ∧
      (∀ p r : ℕ, p.Prime → ¬ p ∣ M →
        T (p ^ (r + 2)) = T p * T (p ^ (r + 1)) - (p : ℂ) ^ (k - 1) • (D p * T (p ^ r))) ∧
      (∀ p r : ℕ, p.Prime → p ∣ M → T (p ^ r) = T p ^ r) ∧
      (∀ (χ : DirichletCharacter ℂ M)
          (f : CuspForm ((CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma1 M : Subgroup SL(2, ℤ)) : Subgroup (GL (Fin 2) ℝ)) k),
        (∀ γ ∈ CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma0 M, ⇑f ∣[k] γ = χ ((γ 1 1 : ℤ) : ZMod M) • ⇑f) →
        (∀ n m : ℕ, 0 < n → (qExpansion 1 (T n f)).coeff m =
            ∑ d ∈ (Nat.gcd m n).divisors, χ d * (d : ℂ) ^ (k - 1) * (qExpansion 1 f).coeff (m * n / d ^ 2)) ∧
        ((qExpansion 1 f).coeff 1 = 1 → (∀ p : ℕ, p.Prime → ∃ c : ℂ, T p f = c • f) →
          ∀ n : ℕ, 0 < n → T n f = (qExpansion 1 f).coeff n • f)) := by
  sorry
